  "articleBody": "Navigating the world of home accessibility can feel a bit overwhelming, right? You might be wondering if you need a full-blown wheelchair ramp or if a simple threshold ramp will do the trick. Let's break it down! Wheelchair ramps are generally for bigger height differences, like getting into your house from the driveway or across a few steps. They'll need more space to make sure the slope isn't too steep – a good rule of thumb is one foot of ramp for every inch of rise. They can also require permits depending on where you live, especially if they're a permanent structure. On the other hand, threshold ramps are usually for much smaller bumps, like that little lip at your doorway, often in the one to three-inch range. They’re super helpful for getting over those interior or exterior door frames without a big project. You usually won't need a permit for these since they're often not permanent. When you're dealing with anything beyond a tiny rise, or if you're unsure about local codes, it's really smart to chat with a professional. They can help you figure out the best fit for your home and ensure everything's safe and compliant.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}